diff --git a/core/runtime/v2/v86/README.md b/core/runtime/v2/v86/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..675d573
--- /dev/null
+++ b/core/runtime/v2/v86/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+# v86 Runtime v2 Shim
+
+A containerd [Runtime v2 shim](../../README.md) that runs each task in a
+[v86](https://github.com/copy/v86) virtual machine instead of a runc container.
+It is the "runc replacement" for the tabernetes control plane: containerd keeps
+managing images, snapshots and tasks unchanged, and this package translates the
+task lifecycle onto the v86 VM of the tab node.
+
+## The seam
+
+`Runtime` / `Handle` are the only parts that know about the VM:
+
+- `Runtime.Create(ctx, id, spec)` boots a VM for an OCI runtime spec but does
+  not run its entrypoint yet. On js/wasm this boots the kernel to the initramfs
+  "ready" state (stage 0 in `vm/init/init.c`), the same point where the tab
+  node waits for the pod before `go`.
+- `Handle.Start` runs the entrypoint (`go`).
+- `Handle.Wait` returns the exit code.
+- `Handle.Exec` / `Resize` / `Kill` / `State` / `Delete` map to the VM's exec
+  protocol (`k8s/tabernetes/v86exec.go`), the tty, a signal to PID 1, the state
+  and `worker.terminate()`.
+
+The js/wasm implementation of `Runtime` lives in the tabernetes repository and
+drives the VM worker over `syscall/js`; `fake/` is an in-memory implementation
+for tests.
+
+## Not supported
+
+The operations that only make sense for a real Linux process are no-ops or
+return `ErrNotImplemented`: `Pause`/`Resume` (no cgroup freezer), `Checkpoint`,
+`Update`, `Stats`, `CloseIO` (stdio streams over the VM, not fifos), `Pids`
+(no host pids). `Connect` reports PID 1, the task in its VM.
+
+## Integration
+
+On js/wasm there is no `fork` and no unix socket, so this shim is not a separate
+binary forked by containerd. It is registered in-process as a
+`plugins.TTRPCPlugin` with id `"task"` (see `core/runtime/v2/example/example.go`
+for the pattern), and its control channel runs over the in-process fake TCP
+network instead of a unix socket.
